WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
Take the calcium away, and this sheet of cells comes apart.

2
00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:06.500
What was the calcium holding together?

3
00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.080
Put it back, and the cells knit together again.

4
00:00:13.567 --> 00:00:16.787
Each cell reaches across with arms called cadherins.

5
00:00:18.207 --> 00:00:21.007
Calcium sits at the joints of each arm and keeps it stiff.

6
00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:24.927
The arm on one cell grips the arm on the next.

7
00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:27.810
Follow one arm into the cell.

8
00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:34.310
Adaptor proteins grip its tail and pass the hold inward: beta β-catenin, then alpha

9
00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:38.610
α-catenin. They tie it to a cable, a filament of actin.

10
00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:41.650
This grip is an adherens junction.

11
00:00:42.783 --> 00:00:46.543
So, in its simplest form, one grip is a chain of four links.

12
00:00:47.263 --> 00:00:52.583
A partner outside, a receptor in the membrane, adaptors, and a cable inside.

13
00:00:53.717 --> 00:00:55.017
Now pull on the sheet.

14
00:00:56.337 --> 00:01:00.857
In this simple picture, the pull runs from cable to cable, through every link.

15
00:01:02.097 --> 00:01:04.197
Break any one link, and this path is cut.

16
00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:06.970
Now take the calcium away.

17
00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:09.690
Which link gives way first?

18
00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:13.650
The arms outside, the adaptors, or the cable?

19
00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:16.170
Choose one.

20
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.200
Calcium leaves the joints, and the arms go floppy.

21
00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:26.860
The outer link lets go.

22
00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:32.900
The adaptors and the cable are still there, but this contact can no longer pass the

23
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:37.140
pull across. One broken link, and the sheet comes apart.

24
00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:42.870
Now look down, at the matrix, the material cells secrete around themselves.

25
00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:48.470
Here the receptor is an integrin, a pair of subunits, α and β.

26
00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:53.930
It grips a matrix protein, fibronectin, at a short tag, RGD.

27
00:01:54.983 --> 00:02:01.423
Inside, talin ties the integrin's tail to actin, and vinculin adds extra ties to the

28
00:02:01.423 --> 00:02:04.243
same cable. Notice the pattern.

29
00:02:05.443 --> 00:02:08.423
The receptor has changed, but the four links have not.

30
00:02:09.703 --> 00:02:13.343
When the cell tugs on its cable, the pull runs down into the matrix.

31
00:02:14.417 --> 00:02:18.237
Two more junctions build the same chain with a different cable.

32
00:02:19.697 --> 00:02:21.257
Desmosomes join cell to cell.

33
00:02:22.697 --> 00:02:27.057
Hemidesmosomes pin a cell to the basement membrane, the thin matrix sheet beneath.

34
00:02:28.417 --> 00:02:33.157
In epithelia, both end on keratin, an intermediate filament, not on actin.

35
00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:36.910
At the chain's far end is the matrix itself.

36
00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:41.770
Its collagen ropes straighten under a pull and resist it strongly.

37
00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:46.050
But squeeze the tissue, and the ropes buckle and go slack.

38
00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:48.430
Ropes are poor at pushing.

39
00:02:49.533 --> 00:02:55.033
So the matrix also holds a gel of proteoglycans, proteins with long sugar chains.

40
00:02:56.313 --> 00:03:01.193
In a first picture, the chains' negative charges draw in water, and the gel swells.

41
00:03:02.573 --> 00:03:04.033
Squeeze it, and it pushes back.

42
00:03:05.067 --> 00:03:09.367
The tissue is one web of chains, from matrix ropes to cell cables.

43
00:03:10.667 --> 00:03:14.467
A real junction holds many chains side by side, sharing the load.

44
00:03:15.767 --> 00:03:16.927
One question is left.

45
00:03:18.167 --> 00:03:19.887
Does the pull change the links themselves?
